// Provisional English translations made from unverified Chinese image-reading drafts.
// These translations are not a diplomatic edition. Do not infer missing glyphs.
window.MIAO_ENGLISH_DRAFTS = {
  "new-qiansheng-plate001": {
    "translation": "The Yangdong Luohan Miao live in the jurisdiction of Liping Prefecture. Men farm and trade. Women wear their hair loosely, with a wooden comb above the forehead. Wealthier women have interlinked gold or silver earrings, short close-fitting garments, and embroidered panels on the chest embellished with silver. They wear long skirts and short trousers, or long skirts without trousers. They raise silkworms and weave silk. The account praises their diligence and cleanliness, noting that they bathe daily. [Several characters describing hair and clothing remain uncertain.]\n\nVerse (provisional): When farming is done, they trade for their livelihood; linked ornaments brighten their chests. Especially fond of cleanliness, they wash their dark hair in the stream.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate002": {
    "translation": "The Guoluan Gelao live in Pingyuan Prefecture. Men weave their own twilled cloth for clothing. Women wrap blue headcloths around their hair in a ring shaped like a cooking pot and wear short blue jackets. When someone is ill, they do not administer medicine; instead, they decorate a tiger's head with colored threads, place it in a winnowing basket, and invite a ritual specialist to pray on the patient's behalf. The account describes them as fond of alcohol and neglectful of agriculture.\n\nVerse (provisional): A tiger is prepared for a ritual specialist; drinking is preferred to farmwork. They weave twilled cloth for clothing, and a pot-shaped wrap encircles their hair. [The opening of the verse is uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate003": {
    "translation": "The Pipao Gelao live in Huangping. Men and women wear garments said to be about a chi long, with an outer cape shorter in front than behind. The description of the pleated garment and its woolen material is uncertain. The text calls them honest and says many make a living by growing pears.\n\nVerse (provisional): A cape covers the head, shoulders, and back; wool and cloth are used for clothing. The inner garments of men and women are short; the final line about their livelihood remains uncertain.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate004": {
    "translation": "The Getong Miao live in Libo County. Men are skilled at cultivation and women at spinning and weaving. Their short garments and skirts barely cover the knees. At a parent's death, the body is placed between wooden boards rather than in a coffin. The text reports singing in place of weeping at first, followed by mourning at the grave for three days. [Some details of the burial passage are uncertain.]\n\nVerse (provisional): They spin cotton and cultivate the fields; flowered skirts reach above the knees. At the death of parents they sing [uncertain words]; after three days by the grave they return home.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate005": {
    "translation": "The Bo people are said to live in the camps and administrative districts of Pu'an. Men and women wear felt garments and, according to this account, do not wash. The text associates them with Buddhism and says they serve as interpreters among groups whose languages are not mutually intelligible, including the Luoluo and Zhongjia.\n\nVerse (provisional): The Bo people revere the Buddha and interpret the speech of different groups. Men and women wear felt clothing [the last line is uncertain].",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate006": {
    "translation": "The group called Manren in this account lives in Xintian and Danjiang, with related customs also reported from Sinan and Yanhe. Men wear straw rain capes; women wear short blue garments with patterned fabric. The text describes singing and dancing in funeral or sacrificial practices, fishing and hunting, and a major festival on the first day of the tenth lunar month involving offerings to spirits. [Some ritual terminology is uncertain.]\n\nVerse (provisional): Singing and dancing mark a rite [opening words uncertain]; men weave their own rain capes. The festival falls in the tenth month, and women's skirts are embroidered with colored borders.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate007": {
    "translation": "The Xi Miao include people with the surnames Ma, Xie, He, Luo, and Jia, living under Guiyang and Pingyue. The account describes marriage arrangements in which couples first live separately and then together after children are born. At the autumn harvest, cattle are gathered outdoors for a three-day communal rite involving a ritual singer in a broad felt garment, a pleated skirt, and a felt hat. Young men and women sing, dance, and play the lusheng. The rite both celebrates a good harvest and honors the White Tiger. The author characterizes the people as straightforward and law-abiding.\n\nVerse (provisional): In the tenth month the Xi Miao delight in song; a ritual leader stands before them in felt clothing and hat. Women live separately before children are born; they play the lusheng and dance over the hillside in a tiger rite.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate008": {
    "translation": "The Nong Miao live at Yongfeng, Luohu, and Danjiang. Formerly administered under Guangxi, these places were transferred to Guizhou in the eighth year of the Yongzheng reign (1730). The people work diligently at farming. The account says men's appearance and dress resemble those of Han people, while women retain long loose hair, short upper garments, and long skirts.\n\nVerse (provisional): Their old homeland was in western Guangdong/Guangxi; they have long since come under southern Guizhou. The men scarcely differ in appearance from Han people, while the women preserve older Miao styles.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate009": {
    "translation": "The Daya Miao live in the jurisdictions of Qianxi, Pingyue, and Qingzhen. According to the source, women about to marry have their two front teeth removed to avert harm to their husbands' families. Their hair is combed forward and falls behind. The account also calls them Gelao, says they comprise five branches that do not intermarry, and describes their distinctive dress. [The final clothing description is incomplete.]\n\nVerse (provisional): With loose hair and bare feet, they follow Miao customs; before marriage two front teeth are struck out. The act is said to protect the husband's family; hair falls to the brows and behind.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate010": {
    "translation": "The Jianfa Gelao live in Pingyuan, Guiding, and Shibing and are also called the 'hair-cutting Miao.' Men and women keep a short tuft of hair on the crown, farm diligently, and, according to the source, cremate the dead on a funeral pyre.\n\nVerse (provisional): A short tuft remains on the head; they farm diligently and store grain. The Gelao burn the deceased on piled firewood; the final words of the verse are uncertain.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate011": {
    "translation": "The Zhushi Gelao are recorded in Shiqian, Liping, Guzhou, Pingyuan, and Qingping. In strongly derogatory language, the text claims that they seldom wash, hunt and eat animals voraciously, carry knives and crossbows, and pursue revenge. It describes feasts offered to powerful allies and states that those in Qingping understand Chinese and submit to local regulations.\n\nVerse (provisional): They are said to risk their lives to avenge feuds, always carrying bows and crossbows. The poem disparages their lack of bathing and compares their eating to that of beasts. [One character is uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate012": {
    "translation": "The Hong Gelao live in Guangshun, Pingyuan, Qingping, and surrounding areas. According to the source, after a relative dies, the body is placed in a coffin but not buried; the coffin is set in a rock cave or beside a large river, sometimes near trees and without an outer shelter.\n\nVerse (provisional): After a parent's death, the coffin is not taken up the mountain for burial; it may be placed by a cliff or a river bend. Such coffins are left without covers, the poem says, in a custom it attributes to Guizhou's non-Han peoples.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate013": {
    "translation": "The Hua Gelao, also called Gedou Miao, live in Shibing, Longquan, and Huangping. The author describes men as disinclined to farm and fond of hunting deer and trapping birds. Women embroider their cuffs in five colors and wear silver ornaments. The passage also mentions rearing silkworms and winding cocoons into beadlike strings, and says the Gelao have five branches.\n\nVerse (provisional): This flower-adorned Gelao group hunts deer and catches birds across the hills. Women wear cocoon-like ornaments strung as pearls and embroidery in five colors.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate014": {
    "translation": "The 'Turen' (local people) are found in many places. Those in Guiyang and Guangshun intermarry with military households and commoners. They sing to one another while planting fields. At the start of the year, they perform nuo exorcistic rituals, beat drums, and sing hymns to the spirits; households they visit provide food and drink. The author calls those living near water gentle and honest.\n\nVerse (provisional): Beating old drums, they sing sacred songs; at New Year they perform nuo rites. Every household they visit offers wine and meat, as the local people pray for an abundant grain harvest.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate015": {
    "translation": "The Dong people are said to live mainly downstream, especially around Hongzhou. The source characterizes them as suspicious of others and says husbands and wives go out together. It refers to preparing salt and sauces with their food and gathering reed flowers in winter to keep warm.\n\nVerse (provisional): In winter they gather reeds against the cold; the poem reproaches their suspicion and quarrels. Many in Hongzhou farm diligently, while a woman in a flowered skirt stands by a railing.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate016": {
    "translation": "The Liao are said to have migrated from Guangxi into Guizhou during the Yongzheng reign, settling in Guiding, Qingping, Dushan, and elsewhere, though often without fixed residences. They channel stream water to their homes through lengths of tree bark. Outside the farming season, they gather medicinal plants, treat illnesses, and practice medicine. They worship a deity named Panhu and preserve a treasured written text whose seal-script characters, the author says, are not understood by outsiders. The account praises their honesty, claiming that lost property is left untouched.\n\nVerse (provisional): They do not pick up what others have dropped. Their origins are traced to Guangxi; selling medicines and treating illness sustain them. They revere Panhu and are described as upright.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate017": {
    "translation": "The Yangbao Miao live in the jurisdictions of Zunyi and Longli. They use marriage intermediaries and observe mourning rituals. The author characterizes them as evasive toward officials: those summoned by the authorities may refuse to appear, yet they heed the instructions of respected village elders.\n\nVerse (provisional): They will not readily enter the magistrate's office, preferring village elders to judge disputes. Their funerary rites express grief, and the final image invokes the cuckoo flower.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate018": {
    "translation": "The group labeled Zhong/Zhuang Miao is found in Duyun, Liping, Shiqian, Shibing, Longli, and Longquan. Common surnames include Yang, Zhang, Shi, and Ou. Men cultivate enough land to feed their households and women weave enough cloth to clothe them. They fish and hunt when free. Marriage and funerals involve substantial gifts or feasts. The description says house walls are left unplastered and doors unlatched, sometimes secured with mud.\n\nVerse (provisional): Marriages and funerals require abundant food; households cultivate according to the number of mouths. They hunt and fish in their leisure time and rely on mud rather than bolts to close their doors.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate019": {
    "translation": "The group called Bai'erzi lives in Weining and neighboring Yunnan. The account says that men dress much like Han people, while women's clothing retains local Miao styles. It traces the name to households formed when Han men married into Miao families, sometimes subsequently returning to Han communities and leaving mothers and children behind.\n\nVerse (provisional): Men follow Han styles and women retain Miao dress. Marriage into another family creates households of different surnames; the final line explains their name.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate020": {
    "translation": "The Bai Longjia live in Dading and Pingyuan. They wear white clothing and often travel into the mountains to collect lacquer, bringing it to market. They carry goods on their backs. The account says their marriage and funeral practices follow Han conventions.\n\nVerse (provisional): In Dading and Pingyuan, the Bai Longjia carry goods and collect lacquer [possibly tea]. They trade for a living and follow Han customs, with men and women dressed in white.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate021": {
    "translation": "The Bai Zhongjia live in Libo County. Men are described as wearing fox tails on their heads, while women wear light-blue garments, finely pleated cloud-patterned skirts, red embroidered shoes, and silver neck rings. At spring gatherings, unmarried men and women play games involving bamboo sticks and a drumlike sound. The account describes courtship in the presence of parents and calls some outsiders who join in 'wailang.' A length of cloth called 'duanlang money' is mentioned in connection with breaking off a relationship. [Several sentences about the festival and marriage arrangements are corrupt or uncertain in the Chinese draft.]\n\nVerse (provisional): Women are praised for their appearance, and young couples play together [words unclear]. Parents watch without forbidding the game; the last line refers to ending a relationship with an outsider.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate022": {
    "translation": "The Tu Gelao live in Weining. Men are said to wear clothing made of grasses and to work as hired laborers for the Luoluo. The source also claims that they apply heated tung oil to their feet and thus travel quickly in the mountains.\n\nVerse (provisional): The Gelao work as hired laborers among the Luoluo; their grass garments require no stitching. The poem repeats the story of hot oil applied to the feet and their agility in the mountains.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate023": {
    "translation": "The Eque Miao live around Guiyang Prefecture. Women wear white cloth garments edged around the chest, sleeves, and hem. They prefer mountain settlements and grow millet and sorghum for food. The text explains their name by likening their speech to birdsong. In disputes or official matters, it says, they follow the advice of village elders.\n\nVerse (provisional): Their voices are compared to birds and their sleeves are white. They cultivate crops in high mountains and turn to village elders to settle affairs.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate024": {
    "translation": "The Hulu Miao live in two localities, one of whose names is uncertain, and in Luohu. The source portrays them in hostile terms as violent, prone to robbery, and neglectful of agriculture, but adds that stricter contemporary laws have led them to observe their duties.\n\nVerse (provisional): When the crops are nearly ready for harvest, the poem accuses the group of assembling to steal rather than tending their own fields. [Parts of the verse are unclear.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate025": {
    "translation": "The Hongzhou Miao live in Liping Prefecture. The men farm industriously, much like their Han neighbors, while women excel in spinning and weaving. Their fine ramie cloth is widely sold in markets and is known as 'Hongzhou ramie cloth.'\n\nVerse (provisional): Hongzhou cotton and ramie textiles are praised for their quality. Women weave, men farm, and the poem describes women singing to the sound of the lusheng.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate026": {
    "translation": "The Xixi Miao live in Tianzhu County. Women wear skirts ending above the knees and bind blue cloth around their waists. Unmarried young men and women gather outdoors carrying baskets, singing and dancing. Those who fall in love meet to drink in the fields. According to the text, unions may be formed before a formal betrothal offering of cattle after a child is born.\n\nVerse (provisional): Songs and dances echo across the Xixi fields. The poem disapprovingly describes unregulated courtship, as young men and women carry baskets through the mountains in spring.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate027": {
    "translation": "The Chezai Miao live around Guzhou. The men practice various crafts and the women excel at needlework. Unmarried couples gather at open-air 'moon grounds' to sing and dance, choosing partners through mutual affection in a practice called tiaoyue ('moon dancing'). The source traces the community to six hundred soldiers of Ma Sanbao who reportedly joined local Miao families.\n\nVerse (provisional): Six hundred soldiers of the Ma family married into local households and learned to play the lusheng. Their moon dances and songs were praised, and loving couples chose one another.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate028": {
    "translation": "The group historically called Sheng Miao is located around Guyang, Huangping, Kaili, and Shibing. The source portrays them as 'wild' and explains the label by alleging that they prefer fish, birds, and other meat barely cooked and still bloody.\n\nVerse (provisional): The poem repeats the author's description of the Sheng Miao as people who carry knives and spears and hunt wild animals in the mountains. [The last line is uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate029": {
    "translation": "The Heijiao Miao live around Qingjiang and Guyang. The men wear short garments and put white feathers in their hair. In the author's hostile account, they carry weapons in groups and used to raid others. To divine auspicious and inauspicious outcomes, they place two shells or snails in water and observe their movements. The account describes restrictions on widows' remarriage and says these customs have since changed.\n\nVerse (provisional): The poem depicts armed raiding parties and describes divination with dark snails in water.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate030": {
    "translation": "The Heilou Miao live in the Eight Stockades of Qingjiang. Neighboring villages build tall structures on open ground called gathering halls, each equipped with a long wooden drum. When disputes occur, the drum summons armed villagers, who listen to the village head's judgment. Anyone who fails to attend without cause must pay a cow as a fine. The household involved supplies cattle, wine, and food for the gathering.\n\nVerse (provisional): The village's tall assembly hall has a great drum. At its sound people gather with spears and knives, while cattle and wine provision the assembly.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate031": {
    "translation": "The Duanshang Miao live in the Eight Stockades of Duyun. Men wear short tops and loose trousers. The source describes women's extremely short, heavily pleated skirts and sleeveless garments, using explicitly disparaging language about modesty. It says people gather wild plants for food, enjoy alcohol, and sometimes sleep in mountain hollows or streambeds even in winter.\n\nVerse (provisional): The poem mocks the short skirts, describes winter bathing and sleeping outdoors after drinking, and ends with an openly demeaning comparison. These are the historical author's judgments, not an objective description.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate032": {
    "translation": "The Jianding Miao live around Guiyang. Men and women arrange their hair into pointed topknots and celebrate a major festival on the first day of the eleventh lunar month. The account says married couples cultivate fields together.\n\nVerse (provisional): On the festival of midwinter [ritual wording uncertain], husband and wife till the fields side by side. Both sexes wear pointed topknots, from which the group's name is said to derive.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate033": {
    "translation": "The Langci Miao [group name uncertain] are said to live around Weining. The source describes a form of postpartum seclusion in which a father stays indoors holding the newborn for a month while the mother returns to fieldwork and prepares food for him. It also records a former deathbed practice of turning a dying parent's head backward so that the parent could 'look at descendants,' adding that this practice had become less common.\n\nVerse (provisional): After childbirth the mother prepares food for her husband, who holds the infant at home. The poem also refers to turning a dying parent's head backward to see later generations. [The wording is partially uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate034": {
    "translation": "The Luohan Miao live in the Eight Stockades and Danjiang. Men wear their hair loose behind and adorn their heads with a tail-like ornament. They particularly revere Maitreya Buddha. On the third day of the third lunar month, men and women present food to the Buddha and refrain from lighting cooking fires, which the author likens to the Cold Food Festival.\n\nVerse (provisional): The poem emphasizes their Buddhist devotion and observance resembling the Cold Food custom.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate035": {
    "translation": "The Bai Luoluo are recorded in Dading and Anshun prefectures. The source presents them as a subordinate branch compared with the Hei Luoluo, using derogatory descriptions of their food and ritual customs. It says they sometimes cremate bodies wrapped in grass and refers to a related group in Puding called Ahe. [Several descriptions of cooking vessels and customs are unclear.]\n\nVerse (provisional): The verse recalls an ancestral figure, Jihuo, and the southwestern region of Shuixi, then invokes the old term 'land of ghosts' and unfamiliar writing. The language expresses the historical author's perspective.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate036": {
    "translation": "The Songjia Miao live in Guiyang and Anshun. The text claims that their ancestors came from China's central plains and were exiled to frontier regions in antiquity. It says their language and writing resemble those of Han people, men wear hats and long-fronted robes, and weddings involve the groom's household sending an escort to the bride's family. The source describes norms governing relations with mothers-in-law, men's farming, women's weaving, and the education of boys, some of whom entered local schools through examinations.\n\nVerse (provisional): Once people of the central realm, the poem says, they were exiled among frontier peoples. Yet they maintain farming, weaving, and formal wedding and funeral rites.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate037": {
    "translation": "The Caijia Miao are found around Guizhu, Xiuwen, Qingzhen, Weining, and Pingyuan. Men wear felt garments. Women have tall head ornaments said to resemble ox horns, ornamented with blue cloth and long hairpins. Fathers-in-law and daughters-in-law avoid speaking directly. During the first three days of mourning they abstain from rice and meat and eat millet gruel. The text records a custom of a widow being expected to die with her husband, sometimes averted by her natal family's intervention.\n\nVerse (provisional): The poem mentions avoidance between in-laws, restricted mourning foods, and the disturbing practice of widow sacrifice, from which a woman might be rescued by her natal family.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate038": {
    "translation": "The Kayou Zhongjia live in Guiyang, Anshun, Xingyi, Pingyue, and surrounding areas. They wear blue clothing, and women cover their heads with patterned scarves, short fitted tops, and finely pleated skirts with cloud motifs. The sixth day of the sixth month is a major festival. At spring gatherings, unmarried men and women dance, sing, and exchange colored balls or cloth as signs of affection. The account describes cattle, wine, and shared feasts as part of marriage transactions. [Some wording concerning betrothal payments is uncertain.]\n\nVerse (provisional): Young women and men toss colored balls amid lusheng music and moon dances. Their clothing changes little with the seasons, and cattle are offered in funeral rites.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate039": {
    "translation": "The Pulong Zhongjia are recorded in Guiyang, Dingfan, Guangshun, Xingyi, and Anshun. Their principal festival falls on the first day of the twelfth lunar month, when they play bronze drums, sometimes said to have been left by Zhuge Liang. Wealthy households purchase drums at high prices. At funerals cattle are slaughtered, relatives gather, and wine is served from ox horns. Mourners avoid meat and eat fish and shrimp; fish and birds are used in sacrifices. An umbrella is placed over the grave and burned a year later. The author claims that formerly they avenged insults with weapons but increasingly obeyed official regulations.\n\nVerse (provisional): The bronze drums sound, wine is served from ox horns, and former feuds are contrasted with recent submission to magistrates.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate040": {
    "translation": "The Qing Zhongjia live around Qingjiang, Guzhou, and Danjiang. They wear blue headcloths and clothing. Women are said to excel at embroidery and games involving boards or balls. A chosen lover is called a 'malang.' Couples may meet at night, with their parents' knowledge, while avoiding a woman's brothers. Betrothal gifts include cattle and wine. The account states that carved wooden tallies serve as tokens in place of written documents.\n\nVerse (provisional): Women are praised for needlework and games; the poem describes courtship with a malang tolerated by parents but hidden from brothers.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate041": {
    "translation": "The Huizhu Longjia live under Anshun Prefecture. Women wear white tops and tube skirts, with square cloth headscarves and a single tail of hair smeared with lard. At celebrations they purchase wine, slaughter sheep, and bring two or three new outfits, apparently as displays of wealth. For funerals the dead are cremated, with the bones gathered for burial. Ancestral graves are honored on the seventh day of the seventh lunar month.\n\nVerse (provisional): Hair is dressed with lard and fine new garments display prosperity. After cremation, bones are buried; sacrifices are made at the graves on the seventh day of the seventh month.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate042": {
    "translation": "The Gou'er Longjia live around Anshun, Dading, and Kangwusi in Guangshun. Men bind cloth around their heads without hats. Women arrange their hair and cloth into two protruding corners likened to dog's ears, and decorate their garments with multicolored beads. After the beginning of spring, a wooden 'spirit pole' is raised in the fields. Unmarried men and women gather around it for moon dancing and partner selection. The account describes cattle or horses paid after elopement, while noting increased use of matchmakers.\n\nVerse (provisional): A white cloth and pole distinguish this village; couples agree to elope. Clothing has its own colors, and cattle and horses figure in the negotiation of marriages.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate043": {
    "translation": "The Madeng Longjia live between the various western forts and Dingying administrative districts of Zhenning. Zhang, Zhao, and other surnames are common. They favor white clothing, changing to blue for mourning. Women make headpieces of fine cloth shaped like horse stirrups. Agriculture provides their livelihood.\n\nVerse (provisional): Families named Zhang, Huang, Liu, and Zhao wear fine white cloth and stirrup-shaped headdresses; mourning clothes change to blue, and the tall headdress gives a stately appearance.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate044": {
    "translation": "The Datou Longjia live in Zhenning and Puding. Men wear bamboo hats. Women wear short skirts and dark-blue or earth-colored tops, with elaborately coiled hair resembling a bowl, from which the name 'large-headed Longjia' is said to derive. The account praises both men and women for diligent farming.\n\nVerse (provisional): Hair is coiled in elaborate knots and blue cloth forms the short skirts. Men and women farm and weave frugally, then gather at markets to drink wine. [Some characters describing the hairstyle are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate045": {
    "translation": "The Hua Miao live in the jurisdictions of Guiyang, Dading, and Zunyi. The source portrays them as simple and law-fearing, describing garments pieced together from blue and white cloth without conventional collars or sleeves. Every spring, unmarried men play the lusheng and women shake bells at a moon-dancing ground, singing and dancing all day and choosing partners. The text describes subsequent marriage negotiations and gifts, sometimes varying with the bride's perceived beauty, and notes ceremonies at the bride's home. [The last portion is difficult to read.]\n\nVerse (provisional): Girls shake bells as young men play the lusheng at dances; the poem also mentions funerary rites and offerings of cattle to spirits.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate046": {
    "translation": "The Bai Miao live in Longli, Guiding, and Qianxi. They wear white clothing. Men are described as bareheaded and barefoot, while women pin their hair into elaborate coils. At ancestral sacrifices, cattle and pigs are selected and cattle may be made to fight; the victor is seen as auspicious and used for divination before slaughter. Participants wear white clothes and finely pleated skirts, then gather with relatives to sing and drink.\n\nVerse (provisional): Cattle are sacrificed to the ancestors and their contest divines fortune. Relatives sing and drink, while the ritual leader wears a long white and blue pleated skirt.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate047": {
    "translation": "The Qing Miao live in Qianxi, Zhenning, Xiuwen, Guizhu, and elsewhere. Blue clothing is favored; women arrange their hair with blue cloth and decorative headscarves, while men wear bamboo hats and straw sandals. The source contrasts an earlier reputation for ferocity with their present diligence and peacefulness. In Pingyuan they are also called Qing Miao ('Miao of the wooded hills').\n\nVerse (provisional): Once described as fierce, they are now said to farm and cut firewood peacefully. Bamboo hats, straw sandals, and moonlit lusheng playing characterize the group.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate048": {
    "translation": "The Hei Miao live across Duyun, the Eight Stockades, Danjiang, Zhenyuan, Liping, Qingjiang, and Guzhou. The source notes many subdivisions and differing customs but says black clothing is common. It praises their agility on rocky, thorny paths while describing a former reputation for conflict and weapons such as spears, crossbows, and swords. Following Qing military campaigns and pacification in 1735, the author claims, fighting declined. In spring, whole villages gather for moon dancing with long bamboo lusheng instruments. At funerals, the deceased's former lovers may wave colored brocade tied to bamboo poles before the grave, and men and women offer rites.\n\nVerse (provisional): Spring rituals seek a good harvest; white feathers and weapons recall the author's descriptions of the group's former customs.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate049": {
    "translation": "The Kemeng Dongyang Miao [name uncertain] live around Jinzhusi in Guangshun. They hollow dwellings into steep cliffs, sometimes reached by bamboo ladders. Farmers are said to use iron tools rather than cattle. The passage describes selecting partners through lusheng dancing and mourning practices in which singing replaces weeping at the time of death. In the following spring, hearing cuckoos brings grief because, as the mourners reportedly say, 'the bird returns with the season, but our parent will never return.' [Parts of the marriage and funerary descriptions are unclear.]\n\nVerse (provisional): Only when the cuckoo returns do they grieve; at the time of death songs and dances took place. They inhabit high caves reached by bamboo ladders and carry water up on their shoulders.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate050": {
    "translation": "The Dong Miao live in Tianzhu and Jinping. They settle on level land near water and chiefly grow cotton. Men's clothing resembles Han dress, and many work as hired laborers for Han households. Women wear blue headscarves and bordered garments. They weave fine Dong handkerchiefs, speak Chinese, and are described as responsive to local authority.\n\nVerse (provisional): Fine woven silk and cotton cloth have long been handed down. They speak Chinese, live on level ground, and cultivate cotton diligently.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate051": {
    "translation": "The Qing Miao [here written with the character for wooded ravines] live near mountain forest gullies in Pingyuan. The account says they cultivate upland grains rather than lowland fields, and that men and women weave their own garments.\n\nVerse (provisional): They prefer planting grain on mountain slopes, dwell in forested gullies far from towns, and weave their own clothes rather than seek urban luxuries.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate052": {
    "translation": "The Dongjia Miao also live in Libo County. Their garments reach no farther than the knees. At the start of the year they offer fish, meat, wine, and rice to a spirit or deity named Yaogu [name uncertain]. They dwell near water and grow cotton, which women spin and weave. The men can speak Chinese but, according to the text, use carved wooden marks instead of writing to confirm agreements.\n\nVerse (provisional): At New Year they offer fish, meat, wine, and rice to Yaogu. They grow cotton, spin and weave, and trust wooden tally marks in their affairs.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate053": {
    "translation": "The Shuijia Miao live in Libo County; their territory is said to have passed from Guangxi to Duyun Prefecture in Guizhou in 1732. Men and women are industrious and frugal. Men fish and hunt, while women's weaving produces cloth known as 'Shuijia cloth.' They wear tube skirts and short tops trimmed with patterned material. At New Year, groups of men and women sing and dance hand in hand, and those who take a liking to one another may become couples.\n\nVerse (provisional): Men fish, hunt, and farm; women weave Shuijia cloth. At the year's beginning couples form amid songs and dancing.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate054": {
    "translation": "The Bai'ezi live around Yongfeng and Luohu. Men arrange their hair in pointed topknots and wear long white garments. Women are described as wearing shorter clothing without skirts. The text compares their customs with those of another group, records prayers to spirits when people fall ill, and says the sick do not wash their hair. [The accompanying verse appears to reverse some clothing details and is uncertain.]\n\nVerse (provisional): The poem refers to pointed coiled hairstyles and petitions to spirits, identifying Yongfeng and Luohu as the group's home.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate055": {
    "translation": "The Ranjia Man live around Yanhe, under Sinan Prefecture. The text says they enjoy fishing and hunting and regard fish and shrimp as delicacies; it compares their customs with those of another group labeled Manren.\n\nVerse (provisional): The Ranjia live in Sinan and spend the year fishing and hunting. The surviving line also refers to cattle and wine, but the poem's wording is incomplete.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate056": {
    "translation": "The Futou Miao live in Guzhou and are described as related to the Dong Miao and classified as Hei Miao. They cultivate without cattle, using iron implements. Their major festival falls on the first day of the eleventh lunar month. The passage elaborately describes women's braided hair, silver head ornaments, hairpins, earrings, neck rings, and multicolored trim. It also describes demanding marriage payments and a fee termed 'nephew money' when a woman seeks to remarry or form a new union under certain circumstances. [Parts of the kinship terms and marriage procedure are uncertain.]\n\nVerse (provisional): The poem refers to marriage gifts and obligations imposed across generations, and to cultivating without cattle. Its last words are unclear.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate057": {
    "translation": "The Eight-Stockade Hei Miao live in Duyun. Women embroider their sleeves and wear decorated breast panels called dianpa. Villages build communal 'malang houses' where unmarried men and women meet in the evening. Betrothal gifts include cattle and wine. The text says a bride may return to her natal home after three days, and that her family can demand further payments from the husband; refusal may lead to another marriage. It also mentions a payment called 'ghost-head money,' though its precise circumstances are uncertain.\n\nVerse (provisional): A malang house is built in the village, and the groom cannot neglect a payment called 'head money.' A bride returns to her mother's home three days after marriage; cattle and wine convey betrothal gifts.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate058": {
    "translation": "The Louju Hei Miao live in the Eight Stockades and Danjiang. Men cultivate the land; the author describes their character as strong and straightforward. Families occupy upper floors of houses while livestock stay below. On death, bodies are placed in coffins and left for a prolonged period, described here as thirty years, before the whole community selects an auspicious date for collective burial. An ancestral shrine called a 'spirit hall' is also mentioned. The account stresses belief in spirits and taboos concerning household objects.\n\nVerse (provisional): They like living upstairs and raise much livestock. The deceased rest in coffins, and the poem warns that improper conduct could bring misfortune.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate059": {
    "translation": "The Heishan Miao live around Guyang and Qingjiang. They bind their hair with blue cloth and dwell deep in mountain valleys. In hostile language, the source alleges that some grow wealthy through capture or plunder rather than agriculture. It credits them with divination using grass stalks to predict fortune and misfortune.\n\nVerse (provisional): The poem repeats accusations of violent raiding, describes dwellings deep in mountain ravines, and mentions the use of grasses for divination. [Several phrases are unclear.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate060": {
    "translation": "The Hei Sheng Miao live in the Qingjiang jurisdiction. The source, using disparaging language, accuses them of forming groups to raid wealthy households with torches and blades. It claims that after Qing military subjugation in 1735 they submitted to government authority and adopted more settled occupations. [Several phrases in the Chinese passage are incomplete.]\n\nVerse (provisional): The verse depicts bands identifying isolated wealthy households, gathering with long spears and sharp weapons, and then turning to farming after pacification.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate061": {
    "translation": "The Gaopo Miao, also called Dingban Miao, live around Huangping and Qianxi. They favor black clothing and cultivation on upland slopes. Women use a wooden piece about a chi long to fasten their hair; this head ornament explains the name Dingban. The passage describes weaving and partner choice outside formal matchmaking.\n\nVerse (provisional): They favor steep mountain fields, and women spin and weave diligently. Couples choose each other, the author says, according to local customs. [The first line is partly unclear.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate062": {
    "translation": "The Pingfa Miao live around Xintianying in Guiding. Men wear straw capes and short trousers; women wear short tops and tube skirts, fastening coiled hair with long pins. The account refers to the use of dogs in marriage, funeral, or sacrificial rites, though the precise wording is uncertain.\n\nVerse (provisional): Men wear straw garments and short trousers; women coil their hair in buns. Dogs are slaughtered for marriage and sacrificial ceremonies, according to the poem.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate063": {
    "translation": "The Hei Zhongjia live in Qingjiang. They plant many trees and the account notes prosperous households. Han neighbors reportedly borrow money or form business partnerships with them. The text describes a practice in which creditors or aggrieved parties might remove ancestors' remains from a guarantor's grave and keep the bones until the guarantor made restitution. Called 'taking the white and releasing the black,' this practice was said to have declined with the introduction of the baojia system. [Some debt and guarantor terminology is uncertain.]\n\nVerse (provisional): Their trading accounts show wealth, and Han neighbors commonly seek loans. When debts cannot be repaid after a fraud, the poem speaks of disturbing burial remains to compel a guarantor to act.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate064": {
    "translation": "The Qingjiang Zhongjia also live around Baigong. In an explicitly hostile description, the source alleges that armed parties once seized lone travelers, placed them in wooden cangues, and demanded money for their release. It adds that recent severe punishment had ended or reduced the practice.\n\nVerse (provisional): The poem condemns forcible ransom, wooden restraints, and armed men with swords. It contrasts those acts with the author's claim that military pacification has restored order.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate065": {
    "translation": "The Liminzi live around Guiyang, Qianxi, Dading, and Qingzhen. Men make a living by trading, while women farm diligently and spin wool into cloth in their spare time. The people are said to value livestock, which they take into the mountains, and to observe festivals similar to those of Han communities.\n\nVerse (provisional): After fieldwork they spin and weave, walking across streams in straw sandals. Men engage in trade around Guiyang, Qingzhen, and Qianxi.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate066": {
    "translation": "The Bafan Miao live in Dingfan. The text says men's and women's dress resembles Han clothing. Women labor in the fields and at weaving, while men are portrayed as more at leisure. Rice is threshed or pounded with equipment worked by flowing water. Relatives gather annually to play long waist drums. Their major festival is the first day of the tenth lunar month. At funerals, burial may take place without selecting an auspicious date, quietly removing the body so, as the passage puts it, the deceased parent is not disturbed. [The agricultural equipment passage is partially garbled.]\n\nVerse (provisional): Women farm at sunrise and weave when the day is done; a water-driven wooden mortar pounds grain. Funerals are conducted quietly so the parent will not hear.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate067": {
    "translation": "The Zijiang Miao live around Qingping and Danjiang and are said to resemble a group in Dushan. The source ascribes to them a former reputation for fighting and fierce retaliation. The first day of the tenth lunar month is a major festival, with taboos about doors. In Pingyue, the text says, some enter military service, fight effectively, or study the Confucian classics and gain admission to local schools.\n\nVerse (provisional): The poem contrasts older ethnic divisions with people now serving in the army or sitting examinations, saying one could hardly recognize them as Miao. [Several historical labels and characters remain uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-qiansheng-plate068": {
    "translation": "The Guge Miao live in Dingfan Prefecture. The source portrays them as formidable fighters who carry knives, spears, and crossbows and are feared by neighboring communities. It also says men now farm and women weave, producing a fine cloth especially sought after for shirts and trousers. Marriages are arranged through intermediaries.\n\nVerse (provisional): Women's spinning and weaving produce admired cloth that sells well in the markets. The poem evokes the men's long spears and powerful crossbows as objects of fear.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate001": {
    "translation": "The Madeng Longjia live between forts and administrative settlements whose place names are not yet securely read. Zhang, Liu, Zhao, Xie, and other surnames are found among them. They favor white clothing; women make fine-cloth headpieces resembling a horse's stirrup. Men and women work diligently at farming. [The first place name and some clothing terms are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate002": {
    "translation": "The Eight-Stockade Hei Miao live in Qingjiang, Liping, Zhenyuan, and Guzhou. Marriage may involve matchmakers, but young people also meet at festival moon dances and in communal 'malang houses' built between villages. The account describes cattle and horses as betrothal gifts, a bride returning to her parents three days after marriage, and payments demanded from the son-in-law, called 'head money.' At funerals, mourners cry, slaughter cattle, beat bronze instruments, and perform rites called 'disturbing the corpse' (naoshi); the dead are placed in coffins without bottoms. The text also records a New Year observance in the twelfth lunar month and a cattle sacrifice to Heaven, Earth, and ancestors on a longer ritual cycle. [Details of the fees and last rite are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate003": {
    "translation": "The Qingjiang native chieftain (tusi) is said to exercise authority over local Hei Miao and Zhongjia groups. The account describes armed men along river crossings who obstruct travelers, allowing passage upon hearing the chieftain's name. Safe passage may also depend on escorts, military protection, or marital ties. The author portrays the customs as threatening and locates them around Qingjiang and Danjiang. [Several administrative and escort terms remain uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate004": {
    "translation": "The Pingfa Miao live under Xiaopingfa in Guiding. Men wear short grass garments, while women wear long tube skirts. The text describes dogs being killed for marriage and spirit sacrifices. At deaths, relatives visit in mourning and coffins are buried. It attributes to the group a former reputation for fighting and carrying long spears, while claiming recent pacification. [Some funeral terms remain uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate005": {
    "translation": "The Guaijiao Caijia live in Dading Prefecture. Men speak Chinese and wear felt garments and socks. Women wrap long strips of cloth around their heads into shapes resembling ox horns, secured with long hairpins, and wear multicolored garments and short skirts. Fathers-in-law and daughters-in-law do not speak to one another. The text makes disparaging allegations about women's conduct and the role of their brothers. [The historical author's prejudices should not be read as factual ethnic traits.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate006": {
    "translation": "The Shuijia Miao live near Zhenyuan and share some customs with the group termed Sheng Miao, though their languages differ. The account says they prefer settlements by water rather than in caves or elevated houses and make their living chiefly by fishing, with some hired work and relatively little farming. It also mentions keeping waterfowl and using fishing nets. [One occupational expression is uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate007": {
    "translation": "The Luohan Miao live under Liping. They venerate luohan (Buddhist arhats), reportedly in mountains or rock caves. Men and women wear fox-tail ornaments on their heads. Men dress in blue, while women wear brocade like the Hua Miao. The author regards their other customs as similar to those of neighboring Miao groups.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate008": {
    "translation": "The Hei Zhongjia live around Duyun, Anshun, and Puding. Men and women wear blue-black cloth. The text says they lend silver and grain freely, even to distant acquaintances, without pressing borrowers for repayment. If a loan remains unpaid, creditors are said to locate a debtor's ancestral graves and dig them up at night. The account claims this practice has gradually changed under government influence. [The tools used in the grave-disturbing practice are unclear.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate009": {
    "translation": "The Xia Miao [group name uncertain] live in Dading. Men wear loose hair bound with cloth, and women wear ornamented combs. They farm diligently. The source describes a sorcery practice called 'burying the soul,' allegedly performed by taking a scrap of another person's clothing and burying it. It also says the people are otherwise gentle, avoid hunting, and sometimes study Chinese writing, although the author claims their speech limits their success in official selection.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate010": {
    "translation": "The Tu [You/Jiu] Zhongjia [name uncertain] live in Xingyi and Zunyi. Men wear locally woven cloth and women wear blue cloth. Courtship is conducted through a ball-tossing ceremony: a woman throws an embroidered ball of five-colored cloth to a man during an auspicious gathering in the open countryside. The couple then exchange gifts and may marry. The text describes this as an old custom.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate011": {
    "translation": "The Duanqun Miao live around Zhenyuan and Sinan. Men and women wear brightly patterned woven garments; women's sleeves are short and their skirts end above the knees. They wear decorative combs and, if wealthy, many silver neck rings. To show friendship or affection, people exchange sweetened balls of glutinous rice. They collect purple dye plants in the mountains and cultivate the land.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate012": {
    "translation": "The group called Manren lives in Xintian and [Danxing, uncertain]. Men wear grass garments, while women wear locally woven skirts. Funeral rites reportedly include slaughtering cattle and singing and dancing. The first day of the tenth lunar month is an important festival, and offerings are made to spirits on specified days. People farm, fish, and hunt, and the source describes them as carrying knives and crossbows. It says the Ranjia Man of Yanhe have similar customs. [Several place names and ritual terms are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate013": {
    "translation": "The Jiuming Jiuxing Miao ('Miao of Nine Names and Nine Surnames') live in Dushan. In a hostile description, the author says individuals may use several surnames or change names frequently, thus explaining the group label. The account emphasizes fighting and blood feuds, adding that conflicts are sometimes ended by intervention from women. Their customs are compared with those of the Hong Miao.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate014": {
    "translation": "The Hong Guoguo live in the Guiyang area. Men and women wear locally woven cloth. Women make tube skirts from sheep or dog hair. The text distinguishes groups wearing flowered cloth and red cloth and says they do not intermarry. It describes coffins placed in rock caves or by rivers rather than buried and structures beside burial sites. The account also portrays the people as fast runners and fierce fighters willing to support others in exchange for food or drink. [Several architectural and social descriptions are difficult to read.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate015": {
    "translation": "The Hongzhou Miao are described as living among other communities in Libo County, which was brought under Duyun Prefecture in Guizhou from western Guangxi during the Yongzheng reign. Clothing styles differ, but languages and preferences are said to be relatively similar. At New Year people perform sacrifices and communal celebrations; couples who choose one another may marry. Women weave diligently. The people are registered for taxation and labor services. [Several words describing festival food and drink are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate016": {
    "translation": "The group called Songren is said to have arrived in Guizhou after wars during the Five Dynasties, dispersing through Pingyue, Sizhou, and nearby areas. Men wear voluminous clothes, felt hats, and leather boots, and women wear long homespun skirts. The source claims their weddings retain older customs, describing escorts of female relatives, weeping before departure, and rituals to avert misfortune. [Several details of the marriage ceremony are not securely read.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate017": {
    "translation": "The Xi Miao live around Pingyuan and Qingping and include households named Xie, Ma, He, Luo, and Lei. They wear blue clothing; men wrap their heads and legs in blue cloth, while women arrange their hair with wooden combs. The source describes couples living in separate beds before children are born. Following the tenth-month harvest, whole villages hold a White Tiger rite in which cattle are gathered and a ritual singer in felt clothing leads men and women playing lusheng and singing for three days before the animals are sacrificed. On New Year's Eve, families also perform a soul-calling rite with chickens and wine. The author calls them honest and generally law-abiding.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate018": {
    "translation": "The Liminzi are here said to trace their origins to Ji'an, Jiangxi, and not to be originally classified as non-Han peoples. They settled in Guizhou, including Guizhu County. Men are described as farming and studying, while women wear patterned headcloths and work in the fields or as hired laborers. At weddings, relatives weep upon parting and escort the bride carrying bundles and umbrellas in a practice called 'following the kin.' The new bride may stay apart for three days, receiving food from relatives rather than her husband's household. The account notes increasing participation in classical study. [Several phrases are unclear.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate019": {
    "translation": "The Yangguang Miao are described as descendants of an earlier population associated with Yang and Bozhou. Common surnames include Yang, Long, Zhang, Shi, and Ou. Communities are found across Duyun, Shiqian, Shibing, Longquan, Huangping, Yuqing, Liping, and Longli. House walls may be left unplastered and doors unbolted, sealed instead with mud. Their clothing and marriage and funeral rites are described as resembling Han customs. Men farm in proportion to household needs and women weave; dogs may be exchanged at marriage and funerals. The author says Chinese-style dress and learning are becoming increasingly common.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate020": {
    "translation": "The Liu'ezi in Dading comprise two branches called black and white. Men wear pointed topknots, and women wear long garments without skirts. The text describes a secondary-burial custom: after a year or so, families return to graves, reopen coffins, wash the bones of the dead, wrap them in cloth, and rebury them; the process may be repeated several times, reportedly up to seven. Illness is attributed to unclean ancestral bones. The author says authorities have prohibited the practice and that it has declined.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate021": {
    "translation": "The Louju Miao have head ornaments likened to sheep's horns, worn by men and women alike. They prefer elevated houses, raise livestock, and wear distinctive clothing: men carry large knives while women wear multicolored garments. Communities are found around Xingyi. The text describes courtship through lusheng playing and moon dancing and says the people also farm diligently.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate022": {
    "translation": "The native-headman group (Tumu) is recorded under Dading Prefecture, with black and white branches. The source recounts a lineage narrative connecting a figure named Jihuo, the Luodian kingdom, and the An chieftain family, invoking ancient campaigns associated with Zhuge Liang. It describes hereditary local chiefs, a hierarchy of offices beneath them, and a written tradition likened to Mongolian script. Men wrap their hair in blue cloth with hornlike folds, tend horses, ride and hunt, and train with weapons. The passage also says the chiefly lineage does not intermarry with commoners. [Place names and some ranks in the source are illegible or disputed; these historical claims are presented as the text's account.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate023": {
    "translation": "The Ma'an Miao live under Dading Prefecture and around Langdai. Men wrap their heads in blue cloth and wear locally woven garments. Women wear multicolored garments and skirts made from patterned cloth. The text describes lusheng playing, dancing, and other spring 'moon-dancing' gatherings in which men and women meet outdoors, similar to Hua Miao customs. Both men and women farm diligently. [Part of the sentence describing women's gestures is illegible.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate024": {
    "translation": "The group labeled Zhuangren lives in Pu'an. The text says men and women wear felt garments and seldom bathe; it also portrays them as interpreters among Guoluo, Zhongjia, and other groups whose languages differ. Their practices are likened to those of another region [name uncertain]. The twenty-fourth day of the sixth lunar month is observed as a festival or New Year, and cooking fires are not lit on the first and fifteenth days of the month. The source associates the community with Buddhist devotion, prayer beads, and the chanting of mantras.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate025": {
    "translation": "The [Ge] Miao [group designation uncertain] live in Zhenyuan, Shibing, and Huangping, especially on high slopes. Men's clothing is described as earth-colored; women wear short jackets embroidered in five colors on the chest and sleeves, with beadlike ornaments on their backs. The text praises their weaving and says they drink wine, carry knives and crossbows, hunt deer, and trap birds. It mentions poisoned arrows but states that they do not engage in robbery. [Several words in the costume description are unreadable.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate026": {
    "translation": "The Liudong people live in Guangshun and are said to have migrated from Guangxi during the eighth year of Yongzheng (1730). The account describes cave dwellings and marriage within their own community rather than with other groups. At weddings, several people escort the bride carrying umbrellas, one of which covers her. The bride's household prepares a full dowry; she may cover her mouth with white cloth while female relatives accompany her. Otherwise, men's and women's dress resembles that of Han people.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate027": {
    "translation": "The Lengjia Miao live around Qingjiang, the Eight Stockades, and Guzhou. The source says couples may choose one another without arranged marriage or a matchmaker and meet in the mountains. A formal betrothal ceremony may follow the birth of a child, with gifts of silver, cattle, or horses exchanged. Both families honor their ancestors as the continuation of the lineage is recognized.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate028": {
    "translation": "The Mu Guoguo live around Guiding and Qianxi and include families surnamed Wang, Li, Jin, and Wen. Couples may sleep apart until they have children. Festivals feature five-colored banners, songs, and dances; the text notes rules of seniority. In some areas clothing resembles Han dress, marriage within the same surname is forbidden, and mourning may involve a forty-nine-day period of remaining indoors without washing. At its end a ritual specialist performs a ceremony called 'releasing the spirits.' The text says young people receive instruction and some enter local schools.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate029": {
    "translation": "The Zijiang Miao live near Duyun, Danjiang, and Qingping and are compared with the Jiuming Jiuxing Miao of Dushan. In hostile language, the account accuses them of fierce feuding and even cannibalism. The first day of the eleventh lunar month is a major festival, accompanied by a week-long taboo on opening doors. It records a practice in which a widow remarries before her deceased husband's burial. In Pingyue and Huangping, some speak Chinese, serve as soldiers, or pursue classical study and examinations.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate030": {
    "translation": "The Maoda Zhongjia live around Xingyi. The source describes a courtship game in which young men and women meet in caves and strike at one another with bamboo tubes or implements decorated with chicken feathers. According to the account, the outcome helps determine a marriage match, and the winning or losing participant must perform a ritual gesture called 'rooster head.' [The details of the game are difficult to read and should not be interpreted literally without checking the scan.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate031": {
    "translation": "The Kemeng Guyang Miao live in Jinzhusi, Guangshun. They carve dwellings into high cliffs, sometimes reached by long bamboo ladders, and cultivate slopes using hand tools rather than draft cattle. Courtship involves lusheng dancing, while betrothal gifts may be exchanged after children are born. The source describes singing and dancing rather than weeping immediately after a parent's death in a rite termed naoshi. Yet, upon hearing the cuckoo in spring, the family weeps, saying that the bird returns each year but the deceased parent does not. [Some agricultural and marriage terms are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate032": {
    "translation": "The Bai Guoluo are recorded in Dading, Shuicheng, Anshun, and Yongning and are compared with the Hei Guoluo. In disparaging language, the source describes a diet without salt and the roasting of small animals or insects. The dead are said to be wrapped in cattle or horse hides and cremated. A related group in Puding called Ahe is described as sharing these customs and earning a living from trading tea. [Part of the dietary description is lost.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate033": {
    "translation": "The Heishan Jiugu Miao live around Kaili and Xinglong Guard. The passage recounts a legend that only nine people survived an ancient southern campaign, becoming nine branches. It describes their customs as akin to those around the Eight Stockades and Danjiang, then gives an unusually detailed account of arms: iron helmets and armor, wooden shields, spears, knives, firearms with lead shot, and powerful long bows drawn by several people. It links the group to Yang Yinglong during the late-Ming Bozhou uprising and describes subsequent Qing military campaigns around 1732, followed by the building of fortified towns and military posts. These military and historical claims require independent corroboration.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate034": {
    "translation": "The Tu Guoguo live around Shuicheng and Langdai. Men often wear straw rain capes, while women weave colored woolen skirts. Both sexes cultivate steep and rocky land wherever possible. Poor households may hire themselves out to Han employers. The source praises their honesty and says they carry medicinal preparations for treating cuts and other injuries.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate035": {
    "translation": "The Gulan Miao live in Dingfan. Men wear short garments, and women wrap their hair in blue cloth. Women's weaving produces exceptionally fine cloth, sought after in markets as 'Gulan cloth.' The author describes the men as formidable with spears and crossbows, sometimes feared by neighboring communities.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate036": {
    "translation": "The Tu Guo live around Dading and Weining. Men wear homespun hemp garments, while women wear small hats decorated with rows of colored beads and textiles. Wealthier people sometimes have patterned trims on their garments. The source dismissively characterizes them as lacking formal learning and etiquette, associating their homes with remote mountain settlements. This is the author's historical prejudice rather than an objective characterization.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate037": {
    "translation": "The Dong Miao live in Guizhu, Longli, and Qingping. The source describes short light-blue clothing, wooden hair combs, and women's floral garments and pleated skirts. Moon dances are compared with those of the Hua Miao. At Mid-Autumn, people commemorate ancestors and dead relatives by raising fat cattle and preparing wine for communal sacrifices, song, and feasting. Ritual specialists call the names of spirits during offerings. Disputes are said to be resolved by village elders, while communities also render labor service to officials. [The long sacrificial passage includes some unreadable expressions.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate038": {
    "translation": "The Hua Zhongjia prefer multistory houses built around a central post, with people living above and livestock below. Incense or household shrines occupy upper levels. The text describes class distinctions between major families in tall houses and dependents in low buildings, including rules governing a local official or elder called binmu [term uncertain]. Wealthy men wear long hair and flowing robes, which the author compares to Ming styles; women wear embroidered clothing with longer back panels. Some households trade in locally woven cloth. The group is recorded around Shuicheng and Langdai.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate039": {
    "translation": "The Qing Miao live around Xiuwen, Zhenning, and Qianxi. They favor blue clothing. Men wear bamboo hats, straw sandals, and sometimes knives; women use blue cloth in their headwear. The text describes settlements near forested mountains, migration, cultivation of beans, wheat, and other upland crops, and the weaving of their own clothes. It also records hairstyles marking unmarried men. The author's claim that the group is combative yet refrains from robbery reflects his classificatory perspective.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate040": {
    "translation": "The Hei Longjia live in Anshun, Pingyuan, and nearby areas. Men wear blue headcloths, homespun clothing, and straw sandals. Women decorate their hair with ornaments called haiba and wear woolen skirts made from sheep or dog hair. The group raises livestock, enjoys songs and dances, and men and women sometimes travel together.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate041": {
    "translation": "The Hulu Miao live around Liping and Sizhou. The source accuses small armed groups of waylaying travelers, stealing valuables, and sometimes causing deaths. It says these practices have recently declined under moral instruction and government control, while continuing to describe the people as fierce. These are claims made by the historical author.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate042": {
    "translation": "The Zhu Guoguo [name partly uncertain] live around Guiyang, Guizhu, and Xiuwen. Men and women wear locally woven cloth. The author describes their physical appearance in disparaging terms. Their livelihood is hunting: on an auspicious day they first sacrifice to a mountain deity, then enter the hills with bows and crossbows to pursue game. Their customs are compared with those of the Hua and Hong Guoguo.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate043": {
    "translation": "The Piqun Miao live in Weining and other localities under Dading. Men and women wear patterned brocade garments and wooden hair combs. They farm diligently in the mountains, carrying food and sheltering in caves during heavy rain rather than returning home. Their name is explained through the skirts worn by both sexes. The account compares them with Dong Miao in Shiqian and mentions cave dwelling.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate044": {
    "translation": "The Hei'ezi live in Dading and Qianxi. Women's hairstyles include coils ornamented with red-and-white beads, large earrings, and garments trimmed with colored designs. The text alleges that married daughters sometimes take livestock or property from their natal households without objection from their parents. Wealthy women wear silver neck rings. The source notes that some younger members pursue education and examinations, but also describes a purported tendency toward lawsuits.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate045": {
    "translation": "The Chezai Miao are said to descend from several hundred soldiers of Ma Sanbao who settled after a campaign near Shuixi and formed households with Miao women. Men and women are described as skilled in playing instruments, singing, and dancing together at social visits. Some speak Chinese but also retain distinct local speech. Men wrap their heads with blue cloth and women wear multicolored Miao dress. The account locates them among six stockades.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate046": {
    "translation": "The Tumu are described as hereditary local headmen under Dading, comprising black and white branches. A lineage account traces them through Jihuo, Luodian, and the An chieftain family and says men bind blue cloth into horn-shaped headpieces. The source praises their horsemanship, hunting, and use of weapons, while reporting that they do not intermarry with commoners. [An extensive middle passage on places and hereditary offices has not yet been securely transcribed.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate048": {
    "translation": "The Jiantou Guoguo live in Guiding. Men and women keep their hair cut to about an inch, and the dead are cremated on piles of firewood. The text also mentions another group, the Zhu or Gou Guoguo [designation uncertain], whom the author depicts in demeaning terms as unwashed and sharing quarters with domestic animals. It alleges that they eat prey voraciously. Those in Qingping are said to understand Chinese and obey government orders; related communities occur in Shiqian, Liping, and Guzhou.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate049": {
    "translation": "The Xixi Miao are said here to live under Dading Prefecture. Men dress in blue cloth and women in multicolored garments. Men and women wear loose hair, play the lusheng, and meet for moon dancing. The author portrays nighttime courtship and relationships in moralizing language and links the group name to these practices.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate052": {
    "translation": "The [Yi/Shuang] Erzi [group name uncertain] live around Dading. The account describes men and women farming, although one phrase about men's work appears contradictory. On the third day of the third, sixth day of the sixth, and ninth day of the ninth lunar months, villages offer livestock and wine before sacred trees. Elderly women arrange their hair in coils. The text also describes servants who are treated leniently, permitted to marry, and given a small share of land, and households living in elevated houses. [Several social and property terms remain unclear.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate054": {
    "translation": "The 'Black Branch' is described as occupying a rank below the hereditary Tumu chiefs. Its members are subject to restrictions on living and traveling with Tumu families, and may be fined for failing to step aside on the road. Elaborate deference is required when meeting a chief. The source also describes a hierarchy within Guoluo groups and a wedding custom in which a bride washes the feet of senior relatives in return for money or gifts. Funerary customs are compared with those of the Tumu. [A passage about marriage sanctions is missing.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate055": {
    "translation": "The Qing Miao live around Guiyang, Anshun, and Dading. Men wear bamboo hats, while women wear patterned headcloths, large earrings, short tops, and long skirts. They farm and, according to the source, some study classical texts. At a parent's death, the body is kept at home for three days and dogs are used in ritual offerings. The text describes an unusual sideways burial and courtship at flower-dancing gatherings, with cattle and wine subsequently offered as betrothal gifts. [The phrase explaining the burial position remains uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate057": {
    "translation": "The Hong Miao live in Tongren Prefecture; surnames listed include Wu, Long, Shi, Ma, and Bai. Their clothing uses red, green, and patterned fabrics. The author claims they prepare meat by singeing off the hair and cooking it only briefly. The dead are placed in coffins, while images or garments of the deceased are displayed during drumming and dancing, called tiaogu ('drum dancing'). On a specified day in the fifth lunar month, spouses reportedly sleep apart and observe silence and seclusion to avert harm from tigers. Women are said to help reconcile disputes.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate058": {
    "translation": "The Bai Zhongjia live in Pingyue, Duyun, Xingyi, and nearby areas. Men and women wear fox-tail ornaments and light-colored clothing and work at agriculture. The source describes a leisure activity involving striking or sounding an old wooden post and contests in which people embrace around the waist and try to overpower one another, with spectators praising winners or penalizing losers. [Several terms for the game and its instruments are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate059": {
    "translation": "The Qingjiang Hei Miao live in Qingjiang, Liping, and Guzhou. The passage describes marriage obligations between related families, including a payment called 'nephew money' when a marriage cannot proceed. It also mentions youthful courtship, mourning at graves, and bamboo poles decorated with colored thread to commemorate a deceased person's former partners. The tenth lunar month begins the New Year. The text portrays divisions between households of higher and lower status and says guests receive wine served from ox horns. [Several kinship rules require close rechecking.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate060": {
    "translation": "The Hong Guoguo live under Guiyang. Men and women wear homespun cloth, and women weave tube skirts from sheep or dog wool. The source distinguishes groups associated with flowered cloth and red cloth, and says they form separate marriage circles. [The middle portion of the original description remains untranscribed; no additional customs are inferred here.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate061": {
    "translation": "The Luohan Miao live under Liping. The account says they revere Buddhist arhats at rock caves. Men and women wear fox-tail ornaments; men wear blue clothing and women patterned Miao brocade resembling that of the Hua Miao. Their other customs are described as similar to those of neighboring Miao groups.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate062": {
    "translation": "The Duanqun Miao live around Zhenyuan and Sinan. Men and women wear embroidered brocade; women's skirts reach above the knees, and wealthier women wear many silver neck rings. Sweetened balls of glutinous rice are exchanged as signs of friendship or affection. In their spare time, people gather purple dye plants in the mountains, alongside agricultural work.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate063": {
    "translation": "The Baizi live around Yijianli under Dading. The source makes explicitly derogatory claims about courtship and sexual conduct in the presence of relatives. It says men trade woolen goods while women spin, weave, and work diligently at farming. These assertions reflect the author's moralizing perspective.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate064": {
    "translation": "The Heijiao Miao live around Liping and Duyun and are compared with the Hei Sheng Miao. Men wear pheasant feathers in their hair and earth-colored clothing; women wear flowered garments. The author claims that before attempting theft, they practice divination with snails and decide whether to steal cattle or horses according to whether the snails open. The passage characterizes the people as fierce and poor; these are historical allegations rather than independently verified observations.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate065": {
    "translation": "The Gutou Miao live under Sizhou. Men wear blue garments, and women wrap their hair in tall cloth arrangements compared with horse stirrups. The people cultivate diligently. Those without cattle are said to plow by human labor, with one person pulling and another pushing the plow.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate066": {
    "translation": "The Guozhu Longjia live in Guangshun. Men wear hemp clothing and women wear a single hairpin in an old-fashioned style, with rows of colored woolen beads decorating their collars. At birthdays of lineage elders, family members present gifts as signs of respect. The account compares their clothing and customs with those of the Madeng and Datou Longjia.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate068": {
    "translation": "The group called Manren is recorded in Xintian and [Danxing, uncertain]. Men wear grass garments and short homespun skirts. At funerals cattle are slaughtered and people sing and dance. The first day of the tenth lunar month is a major festival; offerings to spirits fall on particular calendrical days. The text describes farming, fishing, hunting, and carrying knives or crossbows and mentions Ranjia Man communities along the Yanhe River with similar customs. [Several place names and ritual words require checking.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate047": {
    "translation": "The Chezai Miao are said to descend from several hundred soldiers of Ma Sanbao who settled after a campaign near Shuixi and formed households with Miao women. Men and women are described as skilled in playing instruments, singing, and dancing together at social visits. Some speak Chinese but also retain distinct local speech. Men wrap their heads with blue cloth and women wear multicolored Miao dress. The account locates them among six stockades.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate050": {
    "translation": "The Liudong people live in Guangshun and are said to have migrated from Guangxi during the eighth year of Yongzheng (1730). The account describes cave dwellings and marriage within their own community rather than with other groups. At weddings, several people escort the bride carrying umbrellas, one of which covers her. The bride's household prepares a full dowry; she may cover her mouth with white cloth while female relatives accompany her. Otherwise, men's and women's dress resembles that of Han people.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate051": {
    "translation": "The Lengjia Miao live around Qingjiang, the Eight Stockades, and Guzhou. The source says couples may choose one another without arranged marriage or a matchmaker and meet in the mountains. A formal betrothal ceremony may follow the birth of a child, with gifts of silver, cattle, or horses exchanged. Both families honor their ancestors as the continuation of the lineage is recognized.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate053": {
    "translation": "The Louju Miao have head ornaments likened to sheep's horns, worn by men and women alike. They prefer elevated houses, raise livestock, and wear distinctive clothing: men carry large knives while women wear multicolored garments. Communities are found around Xingyi. The text describes courtship through lusheng playing and moon dancing and says the people also farm diligently.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate056": {
    "translation": "The Maoda Zhongjia live around Xingyi. The source describes a courtship game in which young men and women meet in caves and strike at one another with bamboo tubes or implements decorated with chicken feathers. According to the account, the outcome helps determine a marriage match, and the winning or losing participant must perform a ritual gesture called 'rooster head.' [The details of the game are difficult to read and should not be interpreted literally without checking the scan.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "new-jingong-plate067": {
    "translation": "The Kemeng Guyang Miao live in Jinzhusi, Guangshun. They carve dwellings into high cliffs, sometimes reached by long bamboo ladders, and cultivate slopes using hand tools rather than draft cattle. Courtship involves lusheng dancing, while betrothal gifts may be exchanged after children are born. The source describes singing and dancing rather than weeping immediately after a parent's death in a rite termed naoshi. Yet, upon hearing the cuckoo in spring, the family weeps, saying that the bird returns each year but the deceased parent does not. [Some agricultural and marriage terms are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0002": {
    "translation": "The Qing Miao live near forested mountains in Pingyuan and are described as a branch of the Qing Miao. Rather than cultivating conventional fields, they grow crops on mountain slopes. Men and women generally weave their own clothing.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0003": {
    "translation": "The Shuijia Miao are recorded in Libo, Zunyi, and Tongren, lands said to have passed from western Guangxi to Guizhou in 1732. Men fish and hunt, and women weave fine cloth. They wear tube skirts and short jackets trimmed with patterned fabric. At New Year groups of men and women sing and dance hand in hand; those who become attached may marry. [The name of the cloth and part of the courtship sentence are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0004": {
    "translation": "The Yetou Miao [name uncertain] live around Guzhou downstream and are described as related to the Dong Miao and classified as Hei Miao. They cultivate without oxen, using human labor and sharp iron implements. The first day of the eleventh lunar month is a major festival. Women wear elaborately coiled hair. [The rest of the caption has not yet been reliably transcribed.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0005": {
    "translation": "The Qingjiang Miao [heading uncertain] are described as wearing white cloth around the hair, white rooster feathers, large earrings, and wide trousers. They plant many trees and maintain relationships with neighboring Han households. Following the autumn harvest, villages select festival dates in the tenth lunar month. [Much of the description remains untranscribed.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0006": {
    "translation": "The Pingfa Miao live in the Xintian area of Guiding and around Danjiang. Men wear short jackets and trousers. The surviving text describes women's short tube skirts, but several clothing details and most of the caption remain unreadable.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0007": {
    "translation": "The Bai'erzi live around Weining and neighboring Yunnan. The account says some of their practices resemble those of Han communities. It explains their name through families formed when Han men married into households of Miao women. [The intervening account of trade and customs is fragmentary.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0008": {
    "translation": "The Tu Gelao live around Qingzhen, Pingyue, and Weining. The surviving text mentions clothing made from sheep's wool, settlement in high mountains, and the cultivation of mixed grains for food. [Most other details remain unreadable.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0009": {
    "translation": "The Yaque Miao live under Guiyang Prefecture. Women wear white clothing with embroidered designs on the chest and sleeves. They prefer mountain settlements and grow mixed grains for food. The author likens their speech to birdsong and says disputes involving officials are referred to village elders. [Several descriptions of clothing are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0010": {
    "translation": "The Xixi Miao live around Tianzhu and Liping. Women wear skirts above the knees and wrap blue cloth around their feet or legs. Unmarried men carry lusheng instruments and women bring other items [unreadable] to courtship gatherings. Betrothal gifts of cattle may follow after children are born.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0011": {
    "translation": "The group called Sheng Miao is recorded around Taigong, Kaili, Huangping, and Shibing. In hostile language, the account claims that they eat raw food, carry spears and blades, raid in small groups, and practice divination by observing two snails placed in a basin. It also describes restrictions on widows' remarriage and asserts that customs have recently changed. [Portions of the original caption are missing.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0012": {
    "translation": "The Luohan Miao live around the Eight Stockades of Qingjiang and Danjiang. Men wear fox-tail head ornaments and loose hair and particularly revere Maitreya Buddha. Around the third day of the third lunar month, men and women bring food offerings and sing and dance for three days while avoiding cooking fires. [A later section concerning childbirth and the role of husbands remains unreadable.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0013": {
    "translation": "The Dong Miao live around Guizhu, Xiuwen, Longli, Qingzhen, and Guangshun. The source describes women in patterned garments and short pleated skirts. At Mid-Autumn villages invite ritual specialists to honor ancestors and deceased relatives, slaughter cattle, and arrange offerings. The author describes the people as observing social duties and assisting officials. [Several clothing and ritual details are missing.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0014": {
    "translation": "The Heishan Miao live around Taigong, Qingjiang, and Guzhou. They bind their hair with blue cloth and inhabit remote mountain valleys. [The remainder of the Chinese draft concerning cultivation and alleged raiding is not yet legible enough to translate.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0015": {
    "translation": "The Bai Guoluo live in Shuixi and Yongning and are characterized in the source as a subordinate branch of the Guoluo. The surviving text mentions cooking with a three-legged pot and a related group in Puding called Ahe. It also describes cattle being slaughtered after ritual offerings and meat shared at communal feasts. [Much of the caption remains fragmentary.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0016": {
    "translation": "The Yao people [historical label] are said to have arrived from western Guangxi during the Yongzheng reign. They use connected lengths of bark to lead water to their homes, gather medicinal plants in the mountains, and practice medicine from village to village. They worship Panhu and preserve a treasured text in difficult-to-decipher seal-script characters. The author praises their honesty, claiming that lost property is left untouched. [The name of the book and several other words require checking.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0017": {
    "translation": "The Zhangguang Miao [group designation uncertain] live around Qingjiang, Shiqian, Shibing, and other places. Men cultivate according to the size of their households; women weave enough cloth to clothe them. Fishing and hunting are leisure activities. [The account of marriage, funerals, and the rest of the caption is incomplete.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0018": {
    "translation": "The Hua Gelao, also called Bai Gelao, live around Zhenyuan, Shibing, Huangping, and Weining. The source characterizes men as hunters of deer and birds rather than enthusiastic farmers. Women wear multicolored ornaments that the caption likens to beads. [The last part of the description is unreadable.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0019": {
    "translation": "The Guoluan Gelao live in Pingyuan. Men wear diagonally woven ramie cloth and wrap their hair in blue headcloths resembling the rim of a cooking pot. The surviving text refers to short tops and long skirts, and to calling on spirits or ritual specialists when someone is ill. [The specific ritual details are unclear.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0020": {
    "translation": "The Shui Gelao live around Zhenyuan, Shibing, and other locations. They are skilled at catching fish and can enter deep water even in winter, explaining their name. Men's garments are short, and the passage mentions finely pleated skirts. Marriage and funeral customs are said to resemble those of Han people. [Some clothing and ceremony details are illegible.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-4e93ed1ef413-p0021": {
    "translation": "The Pingzhai or Chezai Miao [first character uncertain] live around Guzhou. Men practice various crafts and women excel at needlework. Young people sing and meet in open-air moon-dancing grounds, even in the presence of their parents. The text traces the community to soldiers of Ma Sanbao who supposedly settled there and formed more than six hundred Miao households. [One word about men's musical performance and part of the historical narrative are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0002": {
    "translation": "The Kemeng Guyang Miao live under Jinzhusi in Guangshun. They carve dwellings into steep cliffs, some reached by bamboo ladders as high as a hundred ren. They do not use ox-drawn plows, instead breaking earth with iron hoes. Young men and women choose partners at lusheng dances, with marriage gifts reportedly exchanged after childbirth. The source describes songs and dances rather than weeping immediately after a parent's death, in a rite called naoshi. Yet at the sound of the cuckoo, families cry that the bird comes back each year while their deceased parent cannot.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0003": {
    "translation": "The Guoluo are described as having female officials who govern affairs in native administrative districts. The passage quotes a long poem by Tian Wen about a female official in Dongchuan. The poet compares the sight to Yan Liben's paintings of foreign tribute bearers, then to a strange dream and a ruined temple populated by spirits and grotesque figures. He describes felt head wraps, curly hair, earrings, a cane, patterned clothing, and a long embroidered skirt, repeatedly contrasting the woman's political bearing with his expectations of gender. The passage concludes that female officials in Shuixi have a similar appearance. [This is a condensed English rendering of an explicitly exoticizing poem, not a line-by-line literary translation.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0004": {
    "translation": "The Hei Miao are described as fighting with swords. Some powerful men wear heavy water-buffalo-hide armor and tall iron helmets that, according to the account, resist spears and blades. The armor is said to weigh seventy or eighty jin and the helmet more than twenty jin. The final sentence appears to list payments or fines in silver for killing, but the amounts and circumstances need rechecking.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0005": {
    "translation": "The Hua Guoluo live around Shuixi in Dading and Yongning in Anshun. Also called Hua Man, they are described as a subordinate branch of the Guoluo. In derogatory language, the account says they cook in three-legged pots and roast small animals without elaborate preparation. The dead are said to be wrapped in cattle or horse hides and cremated. A related group near Pu'an, called Azhi, shares these customs and commonly trades tea.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0006": {
    "translation": "The Xiajia Miao are said to descend from people moved from the south during the Five Dynasties by a Chu ruler [personal name uncertain]. Three branches are named Bulong, Kayou, and Qingxia, found around Guiyang, Pingyue, Duyun, and Anshun. They observe funeral mourning, but the author characterizes them as evasive toward officials and sometimes resistant to summons.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0007": {
    "translation": "The Heijiao Miao live around Goubu and Liangshuijing under Fenghuang Subprefecture. The source accuses them of armed robbery along roads, restraining wealthy merchants in wooden cangues and demanding ransom. It records a military campaign against them in the fifty-second year of the Qianlong reign (1787). These are claims made by the historical author.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0008": {
    "translation": "The group provisionally labeled Linzhu, with the surnames Wang, Li, Jin, and Wen, lives near Guiding and Qianxi. Couples may sleep apart until after childbirth. Rituals use five-colored banners, festivals involve song and dance, and seniority is observed. Clothing in some districts resembles Han dress, and marriage within the same surname is forbidden. The eldest son may mourn indoors for forty-nine days without washing, emerging only after a spirit-release ritual performed by specialists. If he cannot do so, another descendant may substitute. The account says some children enter local schools.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0009": {
    "translation": "The group called Manren live in Xintian Guard and Danxing [name uncertain]. The first day of the tenth lunar month is a festival when offerings are made to spirits; the text also mentions rites on specific calendrical days. The Ranjia Man of the Yanhe area are said to have similar customs. The author uses pejorative terms to characterize the group.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0010": {
    "translation": "The Liu'ezi in Dading have black and white branches. Men wear pointed topknots and women long garments and short skirts. At funerals bodies are placed in coffins. After a year or two, relatives return to the grave, open the coffin, wash the remains until clean, wrap the bones in cloth, and rebury them; this may be repeated as many as seven times. Illness in the family is attributed to unclean ancestral bones. The source says official bans have reduced this practice.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0011": {
    "translation": "The Jiugu Miao live around Xinglong Guard and Kaili and are compared with the Hei Miao of Pianqiao. The account repeats a legend that only nine people survived Zhuge Liang's southern campaign and subsequently multiplied into nine branches. It describes elaborate iron helmets, armor, shields, spears, firearms, lead shot, long bows drawn by several people, and effective fighting on difficult terrain. It links the group to Yang Yinglong's Ming-era Bozhou uprising and recounts Qing campaigns against them in 1732, followed by the establishment of walled settlements and garrisons. The figures and historical claims require independent verification.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0012": {
    "translation": "The Hua Miao come to town markets to sell grain, pigs, and chickens. Women buy small pieces of colored cloth from merchants and sew finely patterned garments, which the author praises as skillful.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0013": {
    "translation": "The Hua Miao in Changping under Fenghuang carry spears and swords. Women spin and weave. The caption describes a sacrificial rite with a white water buffalo fastened to a decorated pole, then killed with long spears; relatives bring wine and gifts. [The final words about the direction of the animal's fall and the ritual's significance are garbled.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0014": {
    "translation": "The Hei Miao live around Yabao Stockade under Fenghuang. They wear short jackets and blue trousers and carry knives or spears. In disputes, they reportedly swear an oath by drinking cat's blood, a practice the author describes as binding among neighboring Miao.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0015": {
    "translation": "The Yao are said to have moved from western Guangxi to Pingfa in Guiding in 1724. They do not maintain fixed residences and prefer places close to streams. They channel water to their homes through joined sections of tree bark. Men and women wear blue garments extending past the knees. They revere Panhu, cultivate fields, gather medicinal plants, and practice medicine in villages. They preserve an obscure seal-script text as a secret treasure. The author praises their honesty, claiming lost property is not taken.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0016": {
    "translation": "The Jiantou Gelao [heading uncertain] live near Guiding. Men and women keep their hair about an inch long, and bodies are cremated after death. The passage also disparagingly describes related groups as unwashed, sharing quarters with animals, and eating prey voraciously. Some are said to resist official summons; others in Qingping understand Chinese and follow village rules. The account names related communities around Shiqian, Liping, and Guzhou. [Several group labels and the final passage are unclear.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0017": {
    "translation": "The group provisionally labeled Zangke are described as descendants of an earlier population associated with Yang and Bozhou. Surnames include Yang, Long, Zhang, Shi, and Ou. They live across Duyun, Shiqian, Shibing, Longquan, Huangping, Yuqing, Liping, and Longli. Their dwellings may have unplastered walls and unlatched doors sealed with mud. Clothing, diet, marriage, and funerals are compared with Han customs. Men farm and women weave; dogs may be given at marriage and funerals. The author says Chinese-style dress and education are becoming more common.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0018": {
    "translation": "The Hua Miao in Majingtan, under Fenghuang, live among other local inhabitants. The text says they study, discuss moral principles, may take public examinations, and engage in market trade with neighboring Han communities.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0019": {
    "translation": "The Yangdong Luohan Miao live around Liping. Women wear their hair loosely with wooden combs above the forehead. Wealthy women wear linked gold or silver earrings. They raise silkworms and weave brocade; short jackets are tied with bands and embroidered panels decorated with silver coins. Some wear short skirts, others long skirts without trousers, with an embroidered hanging panel called a 'garment tail.' The account emphasizes hair washing and describes marriage patterns and relations with another group called Sheng Miao. [The last several sentences are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0020": {
    "translation": "The Dong people [historical designation, heading uncertain] are portrayed as suspicious and quarrelsome. Husbands and wives reportedly travel together carrying weapons; they season food with salt and sauces and gather reed flowers for winter insulation. Communities in the Shiqian and Langxi districts are said to resemble Han people and to enjoy falconry. The account then makes severe accusations of raiding and robbery against communities in Hongzhou, despite noting fertile fields and diligent farming. These allegations reflect the historical author's perspective, not independently verified group characteristics.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0021": {
    "translation": "The Xi Miao live in Pingyue and Qingping, with surnames including Xie, Ma, Luo, He, Lu, and Lei. They favor blue clothing; men wear felt hats, leather boots or go barefoot, and may drape sheepskins over their shoulders. Women coil their hair with wooden combs. The account describes spouses sleeping separately before having children. After the tenth-month harvest, villages gather cattle for a rite led by singers with young participants playing lusheng and dancing for three days before cattle are sacrificed in thanksgiving. On New Year's Eve, households call the names of relatives over chicken and wine in a soul-calling ritual. The author praises the people as honest and rarely involved in litigation.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0022": {
    "translation": "The Guoluan Gelao live in Pingyuan. Men weave twilled ramie garments. Women wrap uncombed hair in blue cloth shaped like the rim of a cooking pot and wear short tops and unpleated long skirts. For illness a ritual specialist is invited to pray before a tiger's head decorated with five-colored wool and placed in a winnowing basket. The dead are placed sideways, reportedly to prevent their return. The source criticizes heavy drinking and neglect of farming.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0023": {
    "translation": "The Hua Gelao [heading uncertain] comprise several branches. One piece of cloth wrapped around the waist forms a tube skirt, and clothing colors distinguish groups with flowered or red cloth who do not intermarry. Coffins are placed in cliff caves or beside large rivers without burial or covering. Wooden memorial structures and small elevated buildings roofed with fir leaves are described. The author emphasizes speed, courage, and feasting in highly exoticizing language, comparing these funeral practices with those of the Bai Guoluo and other groups.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0024": {
    "translation": "The Yangbao are said to be descended from a Bozhou population and to live around Zunyi and Longquan. Their marriage, mourning, and sacrificial practices are compared with Han customs. The author describes them as resistant to official summons in some cases.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0025": {
    "translation": "The Dong Miao live around Guizhu, Longli, and Qingping. They favor light-blue clothing, with women's flowered sleeveless garments and finely pleated skirts. Spring courtship dances resemble those of the Hua Miao. At Mid-Autumn, families honor ancestors and deceased relatives with a carefully selected ox, raised until fat after harvest, and slaughtered for a communal feast. Relatives sing and drink while a ritual specialist calls the names of spirits before wooden boards laid with food and wine. Birds or animals caught during spring hunting may also be offered.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0026": {
    "translation": "The Qing Miao live in Xiuwen, Zhenning, and Qianxi. They wear blue garments: women wrap blue cloth around their heads, and men wear bamboo hats and straw sandals. The author portrays them as combative but law-abiding. In Pingyuan, a related group called Qing Miao of the wooded hills lives in shifting mountain settlements, grows millet, wheat, and other grains instead of irrigated crops, and weaves its own hemp garments. The text also describes an unmarried man's distinctive haircut.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0027": {
    "translation": "The Songjia of Guiyang are said in this account to descend from people of the ancient state of Song who were taken south by Chu and became a frontier population. They speak and write Chinese. Men wear hats and long garments, women hairpins and short-fronted tops. The text describes an unusual wedding in which the groom's party comes to the bride's house and the bride's relatives enact a forcible 'taking of the bride'; rituals of bathing and service to the mother-in-law follow. Mourning may involve a twenty-one-day period of simple food and drink. The author says men and women farm and weave diligently and that some enter local schools. [Some wedding details are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0028": {
    "translation": "The group provisionally labeled Gedou lives around Zhenyuan, Shibing, and Huangping, preferably on elevated ground. Houses have bamboo fences and earthen walls. Men wear plain local clothing; women wear short embroidered garments decorated in five colors on the chest and sleeves and strings of ornaments compared with silkworm cocoons or beads. The account says people enjoy wine, carry knives and crossbows, hunt deer and birds, and possess arrows whose wounds are said to be fatal, yet do not engage in robbery.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0029": {
    "translation": "The Guoluo are described in two branches, black and white, under Dading. The text offers a historical-etymological account of the name and portrays certain features and spiritual beliefs in stereotyped language. It traces the ruling lineage to Jihuo, said to have aided Zhuge Liang, and to the ancestral line of the An chieftains. A hierarchy of hereditary local officials is detailed, including forty-eight divisions, headmen, and nine ranks. Senior figures are said to receive ceremonial insignia and settle important affairs. These ancient claims and some titles require corroboration.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0030": {
    "translation": "The Bo people live around Pu'an and the encampments of local officials. Men and women wear felt garments and are described as seldom bathing. They serve as interpreters among Guoluo, Xiajia, and other communities that do not share a language. A festival or New Year is held on the twenty-fourth day of the sixth lunar month; fires are not lit on new- and full-moon days. The author says they are Buddhist devotees who use prayer beads and chant mantras.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0031": {
    "translation": "The Bafan live in Dingfan. Their clothing resembles Han attire. The source says women work more than men, farming by day and weaving at night. Rice is stored after harvest and pounded in wooden mortars when needed. At seasonal gatherings relatives play long waist drums. Their New Year is placed on the fifteenth day of the tenth lunar month in this version. Burials take place quietly at night without choosing an auspicious day, so that relatives will not be disturbed.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0032": {
    "translation": "The Hei Miao are found in the Eight Stockades, Danjiang, Zhenyuan, Qingjiang, Liping, and Guzhou. The text classifies mountain and riverside settlements under different historical labels, also distinguishing communities under native chieftains from those without officials. Black garments are common; women wear hairpins, earrings, and silver neck rings. Men and women move skillfully over difficult hillsides and farm or gather wood, with women also spinning at night. The passage gives detailed, often derogatory descriptions of glutinous-rice meals, eating by hand, obtaining salty flavor from bracken ash, and preserving meat, fish, and other animal foods in jars until fermented and pungent. It also remarks on scarcity of warm clothing and bedding. This ethnographic account contains authorial judgments and should be checked against the scan.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0033": {
    "translation": "The Caijia Miao live around Guizhu, Xiuwen, Qingping, Qingzhen, Weining, Dading, and Pingyuan. A legend traces them to captives taken by Chu. Men wear felt garments; women use tall horn-shaped felt head ornaments wrapped in blue cloth and fastened with long hairpins, with short jackets and long skirts. Fathers-in-law and daughters-in-law avoid speaking directly. During mourning they eat millet gruel instead of rice or meat; relatives slaughter cattle, gather, play the lusheng, and dance. The source also records widow sacrifice at a husband's death, sometimes prevented by intervention from the woman's natal family.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0034": {
    "translation": "The Madeng Longjia live between Ninggu, Xibao, and Dingying in the Anshun area. The source lists Zhao, Li, Xie, and other surnames. People favor white clothing, changing to blue for mourning. Women's cloth headdresses resemble horse stirrups. Some men study and gain admission to local schools.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0035": {
    "translation": "The Yao Miao live around Pingyue and Huangping and are also called Tianjia. The account claims a descent from the Zhou royal line and lists the Ji surname. They favor blue clothing with left-closing garments, and women spin, weave, and dye cloth. Their New Year falls in the eleventh lunar month; sacrifices to ancestors are conducted by a household elder. The author praises their frugality, peacefulness, and honesty and notes that some study for examinations. In certain mountain districts, the text also describes clothing made from leaves, young women living in bamboo houses, and bodies bound in vines and hung among trees rather than buried. [The relevant place names and final description require checking.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0036": {
    "translation": "The Hua Miao live in the jurisdictions of Guiyang, Anping, Dading, and Zunyi. Garments are made by piecing together cloth strips; women wear elaborate hairstyles and use wax-resist decoration to produce patterned fabrics. At spring 'moon dancing' grounds, unmarried men play the lusheng and women shake bells while singing, dancing, and choosing partners. Betrothal gifts may vary, the author says, according to perceptions of a bride's appearance. At funerals, cattle are slaughtered and kin bring wine; burial practices and divination with eggs are described. Illness is said to be treated through spirit offerings rather than medicine. The passage ends with hostile accusations against certain communities around Zhenyuan and Liping, claiming that military pacification brought them under Qing authority.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0037": {
    "translation": "A group called Turen ('local people') is described in Guangshun, Guizhu, Guiding, and Puding. They intermarry with military families and civilians and observe festivals compared with Han customs. Men and women trade and farm, singing across the fields when planting. At New Year they perform nuo exorcistic rites with drums and songs to spirits, receiving food from households they visit. Near Shibing a ninth-month offering to the Wuxian spirits is mentioned, along with lusheng playing and dancing. The passage also describes distinctive clothing with decorative beadwork, sleeveless tunics, multicolored woolen skirts, and the manufacture of iron plowshares. [Some descriptions of costume and the ritual figure remain uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0038": {
    "translation": "The Hua Miao live in Changping under Fenghuang. Men and women wear patterned garments. Weddings are celebrated by drum dancing and singing. People attend markets and trade with Han neighbors.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0039": {
    "translation": "The Hong Miao of Tongren include people with the surnames Wu, Long, Shi, Ma, and Bai. The source describes silk and brightly colored clothing, meat prepared with little cooking, and funerals in which garments of the dead are displayed while relatives drum, sing, and dance. On a specified day of the fifth lunar month, spouses reportedly sleep separately and avoid speech or leaving home to avert spirit or tiger dangers. Women may intervene to stop fighting. The passage also alleges collective armed raids in earlier times, asserting that government campaigns ended the practice.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0040": {
    "translation": "The Hei Miao live around Yayou, Lilin, and Xinzai villages. The source says they carry spears and swords, wear short jackets and decorated skirts, and train with weapons from childhood. It also accuses them of robbing merchants on major roads and showing little fear of soldiers. These are hostile claims made by the historical author.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0041": {
    "translation": "The Daya Gelao [heading uncertain] live around Pingyuan and Qianxi. Women wear long tube skirts woven from blue wool. Before marriage, two front teeth are removed, reportedly to avert harm to a husband's family. The passage mentions a hairstyle cut in front and worn long behind and ascribes a combative disposition to the group.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0042": {
    "translation": "The You Longjia live in Anshun, Anping, and Qingzhen. Men wear plain clothing. Women wear blue head coverings decorated with ornaments called haiba, and sleeveless or loosely shaped garments that are shorter in front than behind; skirts are woven from five-colored wool. The text praises diligence and reports that many make a living by casting iron plowshares.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0043": {
    "translation": "The Bai Miao live around Guiding, Longli, and Qianxi. They wear white clothing; men go bareheaded and barefoot, while women wear coiled hairstyles fastened with long pins. Spring moon dancing resembles the custom of the Hua Miao. At ancestral rites a strong ox is selected, fattened, and sometimes entered in a contest against other cattle. Victory is interpreted as auspicious before animals are slaughtered for sacrifice. Kin then gather to sing and drink. The author also disparagingly describes instability of settlement and the conduct of some tenant farmers.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0044": {
    "translation": "Six groups [names partly uncertain] are described as living together in Libo County, which came under Duyun Prefecture in Guizhou from western Guangxi in 1732. Their clothing differs but languages and tastes are said to be relatively similar. At New Year they sacrifice to Panhu with fish, meat, wine, and rice. Men and women dance hand in hand; couples choose one another and may marry. They are registered for taxes and labor obligations.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0045": {
    "translation": "The Duanqun Miao live in the Gegang area of Sizhou and the Eight Stockades of Duyun. The source says men's appearance resembles that of the Hei Miao, while women wear patterned cloth wrapped into short skirts. It also mentions an interest in fishing. [The caption is unusually short.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0046": {
    "translation": "The Gou'er Longjia live in Kangzuosi of Guangshun, often near deep woods and streams. Men bind their hair without hats; women arrange coiled hair and cloth into two points resembling a dog's ears, and decorate their garments with colored beads. In summer a pole called a 'spirit post' is raised outdoors, where young men and women dance and choose partners. The source describes cattle or horses given after elopement, then negotiations with matchmakers. At death, a wooden implement is struck while people sing and weep; burial may take place secretly in a rocky ravine. [Several terms about fishing and funeral rites are uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0047": {
    "translation": "The Nong Miao live around Luojie and Ceheng under Yongning and are said to have passed from western Guangxi to Guizhou in 1727. Men are described as resembling Han people in clothing and hairstyles, while women retain short jackets, long skirts, and blue patterned headscarves. The text claims that the area became more settled and law-abiding after replacement of native rule by regular administration, and describes cultivation and weaving amid an unhealthy climate. These are the author's retrospective judgments.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0048": {
    "translation": "The Gulin Miao live in Dingfan. Men wear short garments; women cover their coiled hair with blue cloth. They produce particularly fine woven cloth called Gulin cloth, highly sought after at markets. The source portrays the men as strong fighters armed with spears and crossbows and feared by other groups.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-06b056128ca2-p0049": {
    "translation": "The Zijiang Miao live around Duyun, Danjiang, and Qingping and are compared with the Jiuming Jiuxing of Dushan. In exceptionally hostile language, the author accuses them of violence, feuding, and cannibalism. The tenth-month New Year involves keeping doors closed for seven days as a taboo. The text also describes a widow remarrying before her husband's burial. In Pingyue and Huangping, some speak Chinese, serve in the army, or study for official examinations, prompting the author's remark that they may no longer be recognizable as Miao.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0003": {
    "translation": "The Gulan Miao live in Dingfan. The author portrays them as formidable with spears and crossbows, but says they now chiefly farm. Women spin and weave especially fine cloth known as Gulan cloth, sought after in markets for shirts and trousers. Marriage usually involves matchmakers.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0004": {
    "translation": "The Eight-Stockade Hei Miao live under Duyun. Women wear colored embroidery on their sleeves and two decorated panels across their chests, called dudu. Villages build communal malang houses for unmarried men and women to meet in the evenings. Cattle and wine are exchanged as betrothal gifts; a bride may return to her natal home three days after the wedding. Her family can demand payments from the son-in-law, including 'head money' and, in certain circumstances, 'ghost-head money.' [The last kinship passage is uncertain.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0005": {
    "translation": "The Qingjiang Hei Miao are described as wearing red haircloths, silver neck rings, large earrings, and wide trousers; men go barefoot. They plant trees and trade with Han neighbors, purchasing old embroidered robes at high prices. Young unmarried men and women have special names. On fine spring days they bring wine to hillsides and sing to one another; couples may leave together after drinking from ox horns. The account says men may take up farming after the birth of a child.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0006": {
    "translation": "The Luohan Miao live around the Eight Stockades and Danjiang. Men wear fox-tail ornaments and let their hair fall behind their heads. They especially revere Maitreya Buddha. On the third day of the third lunar month, families bring food offerings, sing and dance for three days, and avoid lighting cooking fires, recalling the Cold Food Festival.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0007": {
    "translation": "The Yangdong Luohan Miao live in Liping Prefecture. Men farm and trade. Women wear loosely arranged hair with wooden combs at the forehead; wealthy women have gold or silver linked earrings. Their short jackets are tied at the back and decorated on the chest with embroidered panels and metal ornaments. They raise silkworms, weave brocade, and wear skirts with or without trousers. The author particularly praises their diligence and frequent hair washing.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0008": {
    "translation": "The Kemeng Guyang Miao live under Jinzhusi in Guangshun, hollowing homes into high cliffs reached by bamboo ladders. They cultivate with hand tools instead of cattle. Unmarried people meet through lusheng dances, with marriage payments reportedly following childbirth. At a parent's death they sing and dance in a rite called naoshi rather than weep. Hearing the returning cuckoo in the next spring, the family mourns, saying that the bird returns but the deceased parent cannot.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0009": {
    "translation": "The Dong Miao live in Tianzhu and Jinping near water and cultivate cotton. Men dress much like Han people and often work as hired laborers for Han households. Women wear blue headscarves and flower-trimmed garments and weave fine Dong brocade and handkerchiefs. The author says they speak Chinese and comply with local administration; related communities are also found in Hunan.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0010": {
    "translation": "The Qing Miao live in wooded mountain areas of Pingyuan and are regarded as related to the Qing Miao. Rather than lowland farming, they grow upland grains, and both men and women weave their own garments.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0011": {
    "translation": "The [name illegible] Miao live in Libo County. The last day of the tenth lunar month is their major festival, marked by offerings to spirits. Men and women wear blue patterned headscarves; unmarried people wear longer scarves. In midwinter, young men and women gather for songs and dances and may form couples. Formal gifts and marriage arrangements follow childbirth; until then, the woman reportedly does not return ceremonially to her natal home.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0012": {
    "translation": "The Dongjia Miao live in Libo County and favor short blue garments. At New Year they offer chicken, fish, meat, wine, and rice to Panhu. They prefer living near water and cultivate cotton, which women spin and weave. Men speak Chinese but, according to the source, use carved wooden marks in place of writing to record agreements.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0013": {
    "translation": "The Shuijia Miao live in Libo, which came under Duyun Prefecture from western Guangxi in 1732. Men enjoy fishing and hunting and women excel at weaving cloth called Shuijia cloth. Their short garments and tube skirts have patterned trim. At New Year, men and women sing and dance hand in hand; couples choose one another and may marry.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0014": {
    "translation": "The Hei Xiajia live around Qingjiang and are said to have become prosperous through trees and commerce. Han merchants borrow money or form partnerships with them. When a partner cannot repay a loan, they may allow additional borrowing. The source describes an extreme custom of removing ancestral bones from the tombs of guarantors when a borrower defaults, returning them only when the debt is recovered. Officials reportedly introduced joint-household guarantees to restrain the practice.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0015": {
    "translation": "The Qingjiang Xiajia also live around Taigong. Women work diligently at farming and weaving. Men wear red headcloths and carry swords. In a hostile account, groups formerly seized lone travelers, confined them in wooden cangues, and demanded ransom. The text says such practices declined following military pacification.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0016": {
    "translation": "The Liminzi live around Dading, Qianxi, Guiyang, Guizhu, Anshun, and Qingzhen. Men make a living through trade, while women farm and, when free, weave woolen cloth. The people raise livestock and take them to mountain pastures. Their festivals are described as resembling Han customs. The author notes that some group names in the collection are newly reported and hard to classify.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0017": {
    "translation": "The Bai'erzi are recorded in Weining and Yunnan. Men are said to follow Han styles while women retain Miao customs. The source explains their name as arising from households formed when Han men married into Miao women's families.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0018": {
    "translation": "The Bai Longjia live in Pingyuan under Dading. They wear white garments, gather lacquer sap in the mountains, and carry goods on their backs to town markets. Their marriage and funeral rites are described as following Han conventions.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0019": {
    "translation": "The Bai Xiajia live in Libo. Men wear fox-tail ornaments and farm; women wear light-blue clothing, finely pleated cloud-patterned skirts, red embroidered shoes, and colorful leg coverings. In early spring, men and women strike a hollow wooden vessel called bazao with bamboo sticks, making drumlike rhythms, and play courtship games. Parents may watch without forbidding them; Chinese-speaking outsiders who join are called wailang. After a woman marries, her husband's family sends cloth to mark the end of these outside relationships.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0020": {
    "translation": "The Louju Hei Miao live around the Eight Stockades and Danjiang. Men farm and women wear hornlike coiffures. They prefer houses with an upper floor for people and a lower level for livestock. The dead may remain in coffins for twenty years, after which villages select an auspicious date and bury many coffins together. A communal ancestral shrine is called a 'spirit hall,' and strict taboos surround the site.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0021": {
    "translation": "The Heishan Miao live between Taigong, Qingjiang, and Guzhou. They bind their hair with blue cloth and inhabit remote valleys. The source accuses them of neglecting cultivation and acquiring wealth through raids; it also says they practice divination with grass stalks. The author notes that they have recently become more peaceful.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0022": {
    "translation": "The Hei Sheng Miao live in the Qingjiang area. The source alleges that armed groups once raided wealthy households, gathering with torches, spears, and knives. It says they were subdued by Qing troops in the third year of Yongzheng (1725) and now obey the law. [This source gives a different date from some related albums.]",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  },
  "ocr-e4a7e4a3b955-p0023": {
    "translation": "The Gaopo Miao, also called Dingban Miao, live around Pingyuan and Qianxi. They wear dark clothing and cultivate hillsides. Women secure their hair with wooden boards about a chi long. The account describes courtship by mutual choice and praises their dyeing and weaving skills.",
    "translation_status": "ai_draft"
  }
};
